BRICKSTOP by Tim Shaw (tim@oasis.icl.stc.co.uk)This is a simple block-catching game for Unix System V.3 or later.The object of the game is to catch the falling blocks with a paddle beforethey build up to the height of the paddle. The speed of the falling blocksgradually increases during the game.Colour is implemented for those that have it. The main block to portabilityis the use of the poll() system call, which is used here to allow a sub-seconddelay.--- Added modifications for BSD Unixes ---I added #ifdefs for BSD to get bs to run on a Sun 3/60 SunOS 3.5.This mainly consisted of using random() instead of srand48(); usingselect to delay instead of poll(); and checking for pending inputwith ioctl(FIONREAD) instead of using nodelay(win) which is notsupported in BSD curses. It appears to work OK, but has not beenextensively tested.	Bill Randle (billr@saab.CNA.TEK.COM)	Apr 24, 1990